<p>US federal prosecutors have charged Army Special Forces member Gannon Ken Van Dyke with using classified information tied to the January operation targeting Nicolás Maduro to place prediction-market bets on Polymarket, according to multiple reports published on April 23 and 24. Authorities say Van Dyke helped plan and carry out the mission, then used that nonpublic knowledge to wager on outcomes tied to Maduro’s removal, making more than $400,000. Some outlets put the total above $511,000. The case matters beyond one defendant because it will help show how far existing fraud and commodities laws can reach when people use sensitive government information on emerging prediction markets. Prosecutors and regulators say the alleged conduct turned a covert national-security operation into a personal trading opportunity. Polymarket, for its part, said it flagged the suspicious activity and cooperated with investigators, casting the case as evidence that monitoring systems can work.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/lARgGPl2QPomDdjjwCJUoqgWAei_u4CfjGkgTV4YB2C4SV5J0OgtWAP52di_LG84rzl8rgmsLRz3TZVOdlT3R9sxXzsAN8nfqYJN86vUDugZYkjdyMrWeePZXMlKXrQxlYYwCDmmmos086feXAnUQoX2AS-lAS9-JeS6sNhYO0ke3GuWu63YhFHOcM9f3sFo3-lbN-ttsek9QoQgPcsyE5kAx8PFn2RTafnzMJcctXAAUO3x74mI5TD5aU3vz4-f8imwy3VYjtHOngu3cg' alt='Captured Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro arrives at the Downtown Manhattan Heliport, as he heads towards the Daniel Patrick Manhattan United States Courthouse for an initial appearance in New York City on January 5, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Bet timing: Prosecutors say he began placing bets in early December, weeks before the January operation, while taking part in mission planning tied to Maduro’s capture.</li><li>Platform response: Polymarket said it identified the betting activity, referred the account to the US Department of Justice, and cooperated with the investigation, adding that insider trading has no place on the platform.</li></ul><blockquote>Our men and women in uniform are trusted with classified information in order to accomplish their mission…and are prohibited from using this highly sensitive information for personal financial gain - Todd Blanche</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>US Justice Department: Federal prosecutors say he used classified government information for private profit and brought criminal charges under fraud and commodities law. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/u-s-soldier-charged-with-using-classified-intel-to-win-more-than-400k-in-bet-on-maduro-raid'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Polymarket: The company said it flagged the account to investigators and argued the arrest shows internal detection and cooperation systems can work when suspicious trading appears. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1994582/us-soldier-allegedly-bet-on-maduro-operation-using-intel'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>News analysts: Several outlets framed the case as part of a broader debate over whether prediction markets are becoming places to trade on privileged geopolitical information. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-soldier-allegedly-made-400k-on-polymarket-betting-on-maduro-raid/a-76918858'>DW</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/doj-charges-soldier-accused-of-betting-400000-on-maduro-removal'>US soldier charged over making more than $511k on Maduro removal bets</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-us-soldier-polymarket-bet-maduro-ken-van-dyke/'>U.S. soldier charged for alleged $400,000 Polymarket bet on Maduro removal</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/23/nyregion/polymarket-maduro-indictment-soldier.html'>Soldier Used Classified Information to Bet on Maduro’s Ouster, U.S. Says</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/24/us-soldier-facing-charges-for-allegedly-betting-on-maduro-operation-using-intel_675